WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.240
Imagine that you've just been hired
to create a database for college.

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.140
First, you need to create tables to
hold data in all aspects of the college.

3
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.812
Then you need to insert
data into these tables and

4
00:00:11.812 --> 00:00:14.900
then modify this data
whenever something changes.

5
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.440
That's a lot of work.

6
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.740
But it's all possible with the use
of SQL and crowed operations.

7
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.440
Not familiar with these operations,
no problem.

8
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.977
Over the next few minutes, you learn how
to explain the tasks that SQL syntax is

9
00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:30.040
used for when building a database,

10
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.740
and demonstrate an understanding of
the SQL subsets and sub languages.

11
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.540
So let's return to our
college database scenario.

12
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.140
How can you possibly make all
these changes in the database?

13
00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:46.340
Well, with the help of what web
developers CRUD operations.

14
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.806
Performing CRUD operations is
the most common task when working

15
00:00:49.806 --> 00:00:50.904
with the database.

16
00:00:50.904 --> 00:00:55.144
CRUD stands for
create read update and delete.

17
00:00:55.144 --> 00:01:00.188
Or in operational terms, create,
add or insert data, read data,

18
00:01:00.188 --> 00:01:05.319
update existing data and
delete data alongside these operations.

19
00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:07.819
There are many other
things that SQL can do.

20
00:01:07.819 --> 00:01:09.780
Depending on what SQL is used for.

21
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.540
It can be divided into many subsections or
sub languages.

22
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.540
These include DDL or
data definition language.

23
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.140
DML also known as data
manipulation language.

24
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.773
DQL or data query language.

25
00:01:23.773 --> 00:01:27.740
And DCL also called data control language.

26
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.044
Let's take a closer look
at these languages and

27
00:01:30.044 --> 00:01:33.161
their commands starting with
data definition language or DDL.

28
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.940
DDL as the name says helps you
define data in your database.

29
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.030
But what does it mean to define data?

30
00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.340
Before you can store data in the database.

31
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.275
You need to create the database and

32
00:01:46.275 --> 00:01:49.822
related objects like tables in
which your data will be stored.

33
00:01:49.822 --> 00:01:54.540
For this, the DDL part of SQL
has a command named create.

34
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.140
Then you might need to modify
already created database objects.

35
00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:58.860
For example,

36
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.240
you might need to modify the structure
of a table by adding a new column.

37
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.840
You can perform this task
with the DDL alter command.

38
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.102
You can remove an object like a table from
a database using the DDL drop command.

39
00:02:12.102 --> 00:02:16.834
Data manipulation language or DML
commands are used to manipulate data in

40
00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:21.540
the database, like inserting updating or
deleting data.

41
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.740
Most crude operations fall under DML.

42
00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:28.740
To add data to a table,
you can use the insert command.

43
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.062
This command lets you specify the fields
to add data too along with the values to

44
00:02:33.062 --> 00:02:34.240
be inserted.

45
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.496
If you need to edit data that's
already inserted into a table,

46
00:02:38.496 --> 00:02:40.757
just deploy the update command.

47
00:02:40.757 --> 00:02:45.554
And you can specify data to be
removed by using the delete command.

48
00:02:45.554 --> 00:02:51.040
So far, you've learned how to add database
objects and manage data within them.

49
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.644
So how do you read or retrieve that data?

50
00:02:54.644 --> 00:02:59.547
To read data stored in a database,
you can use data query language.

51
00:02:59.547 --> 00:03:04.540
DQL defines a select command
to be able to retrieve data.

52
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.006
Select lets you retrieve data from one or
multiple tables letting you specify

53
00:03:09.006 --> 00:03:13.540
the data fields that you want based
on preferred filter criteria.

54
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.039
And finally, you can also use DCL or

55
00:03:16.039 --> 00:03:20.133
data control language to
control access to the database.

56
00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.192
For example, using DCL commands,

57
00:03:22.192 --> 00:03:26.040
you control access to data
stored in the database.

58
00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.230
Grant and revoke DDL commands are used
to give users access privileges to data,

59
00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:35.640
and to revert access privileges
already given to users.

60
00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.307
You should now be familiar with how
SQL acts as the interface between

61
00:03:39.307 --> 00:03:41.044
the database and its users, and

62
00:03:41.044 --> 00:03:45.340
you should also be able to identify
SQL operations and sub languages.

63
00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:45.850
Great work.